  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "Evaluating Large-Scale Emergency Exercises (LSEEs) within complex multi-agency landscapes remains a critical challenge for maritime transport governance. This paper analyses the OMSAR 2024 exercise—a multi-hazard simulation involving a support vessel collision and a mass maritime rescue operation along a critical 175-kilometre offshore transport corridor in Malaysia. Integrating the Five-Element Model of Effective Emergency Management with structured real-time observations from 37 subject matter experts, this study evaluates the operationalization of the Arahan NADMA No. 1 directive under intense logistical stress. Systematic refinement of 152 primary observer logs yielded 51 consolidated findings, exposing a critical disparity between tactical seamanship and systemic transport coordination. Severe procedural bottlenecks were concentrated in Resources & Logistics (25%) and Command Structure (22%), highlighted by a 40-minute ’command vacuum’ during vessel fleet handovers between corporate and state authorities. The findings demonstrate that inter-agency friction between separate maritime protocols undermines the Unified Command required for deep-water transport crises. We offer strategic policy interventions: standardizing maritime command triggers, digitalizing information systems for a Common Operating Picture, and institutionalizing biennial joint Emergency Preparedness and Response (EP&R) transit certifications to safeguard interdisciplinary maritime transport networks.",
